      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Medieval English peasants were legally bound to the land and subject to feudal obligations, lacking the economic freedom to negotiate terms.",
      "evidence": "Historical records of serfdom and villeinage show that peasants were bound to the manor and could not leave or change labor conditions without the lord's permission.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Lords held unilateral legal and economic authority over peasants, dictating labor services and rents rather than engaging in mutual negotiation.",
      "evidence": "Manorial court rolls and the Statute of Labourers demonstrate that lords set the terms of labor and produce extraction, and peasants had no legal recourse to alter these fixed dues.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The exchange of labor and produce was based on coercive obligation and social hierarchy rather than voluntary market-based fair negotiation.",
      "evidence": "The feudal system operated on fixed obligations (corvée, rent in kind) enforced by law and custom, not on supply-and-demand pricing or voluntary contract formation.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, the relationship between lords and peasants did not meet the criteria for 'fair trade' due to the inherent lack of voluntariness and equity.",
      "evidence": "Synthesis of the structural coercion and power imbalance confirms that the interactions were extractive rather than equitable exchanges.",
